the unification of Germany in the late  19th century presented a unique problem  for Britain you see the United Kingdom  wanted little to do with mainland Europe  and it's constant infighting it was only  interested in maintaining and growing  its empire by the end of the 19th  century the German Empire was mainland  Europe's most powerful nation its  emperor Vilhelm ii wanted to increase  Germany's power and build its own global  Empire because prestige and also money  to this end there was one small problem  Britain so the head of Germany's Navy  was a man called Admiral Alfred von  Tirpitz who came up with a great idea  let's begin a major naval buildup but  first I should just let you know that  the purpose of the naval buildup was not  to displace the Royal Navy as the  world's foremost sea power Britain had  to use its Navy to defend its global  Empire whereas Germany could concentrate  its entire fleet in the North Sea this  strategy by the way is known as the  fleet in being doctrine whereby the mere  threat of naval action would be enough  to force concessions with the strategy  in place Germany got to building and  rapidly began to expand its Navy and  Britain for the first few years wasn't  too concerned because its strategy  didn't need to change this strategy was  known as the to power standard whereby  Britain would maintain the same number  of capital ships as the next two largest  navies combined Britain didn't really  become concerned until 1900 when Germany  announced another naval buildup the  British government then appointed a man  called Jackie Fisher as the head of the  Royal Navy to face this challenge and  one of the first things he did was  champion a new type of ship one heavily  armed and armoured whose job it was to  sink enemy battleships this new ship was  finished in 1906 and of the HMS  dreadnought so the fact that Britain had  just made every previously built German  battleship redundant wasn't good news  for Tirpitz nor for Imperial Germany  which was spending far too much money in  all of this in the end the Germans  decided to slow down their naval program  and just kidding they started building  dreadnaughts by 1908 the German Navy had  ordered a dreadnaughts whereas the Royal  Navy possessed one and had ordered six  more and naturally this was worrying for  Britain its concerns were made worse  when considering that Germany's allies  austria-hungary in Italy had ordered  another five dreadnaughts between them  thus Britain was left with three options  one spend all of the money and construct  more warships to come to an agreement  with Germany to slow down production or  three do something no British government  ever wanted to do ally with France and  use her dreadnaughts to balance it out  in the end Britain chose all three it  ordered another eleven dreadnaughts  which in turn would be bolstered by  Frances and as for coming to an  agreement Britain had tried for several  years to get the Germans to slow down  but to no avail  it wasn't until 1912 when the cost of  the naval arms race was becoming  problematic that any agreements were  discussed the issue was that Germany  refused to discuss naval disarmament  until Britain had formally entered an  alliance with it in the end it was the  First Lord of the Admiralty Winston  Churchill who created a program which  meant that for every new dreadnought the  Germans ordered Britain would order to  bust German leaders could see that  continuing towards a new dreadnaughts  would be met with a British response  that would in fact increase the gap  between both countries and by 1914 the  naval arms race was at an end with  Britain in possession of nearly twice as  many dreadnoughts as Germany alongside  the dreadnoughts many hundreds of other  ships had also been built in Germany  opted then to turn its attention to you  boats the naval arms race had severely  damaged anglo-german relations and had  push Britain into an alliance with  France and Russia which as we all know  along with numerous other factors laid  the foundations for the First World War  I hope you enjoyed this episode and  thank you for watching with extra thanks  to these patrons you can see on screen  for their continued support and an extra  special thanks to James Bissonnette  David archaeologists as ARCA Flash Danny  Maloney party-boy Koch coolin Castleman  Rob Waterhouse Chris wicker Gustav Swan  David Silverman Winston Kate woods  Christian checky Maggie packs Kowski  Lexi Schwinn Anthony Beckett  Robert Wetzel Skye Chappell Mike  spinning three plates are on the white  and Michael Reynolds
 

